# Coder OSS v2.34.5 — idle workspace API capacity (EKS)

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Region / account:** `us-east-1`  
**Cluster:** `eks-podman-coder-lab` (destroyed after this run)  
**Question:** How many *idle, connected* standard workspaces can **one** OSS `coderd` hold before the Coder API exceeds a **300 ms p95** budget?

This is a control-plane test (agent ↔ coderd), not nested Podman. It informs ADR-0001 (middle service + standard workspaces): plan concurrent **running** sandboxes, not historical workspace count.

## Verdict

**What degraded:** one API handler on a single OSS `coderd` — **`GET /api/v2/workspaces`** (list all workspaces + live agent status). At 400 idle agents, **every** list during the 10-minute hold took **100–500 ms** (Prometheus p50 **300 ms**, p95 **480 ms**).

**Why that is too much:** the run’s SLO was **p95 ≤ 300 ms**. ~400–480 ms fails that bar. A human opening a dashboard once might still find 400 ms usable, but it is already sluggish — and the **typical** call (p50) is already **at** 300 ms, not only the slow tail. That is with **idle** agents and no real job work. A middle service that polls this list would feel that on every poll. **200** workspaces stayed under budget (p95 **97 ms**). **400** did not.

**What did *not* degrade:** workspace pods stayed Running; all **400 agents stayed connected**; `GET /healthz` stayed ~1 ms; `GET /api/v2/templates` stayed ~24 ms; Terraform/provisionerd was idle; the node groups were not full.

**Cause (from Prometheus, not a guess about CPU):** each idle workspace holds two long-lived connections **on that same coderd process** (agent RPC websocket + embedded DERP). Listing workspaces has to walk that live set. The SQL for the list is still ~11 ms; the extra hundreds of milliseconds are **in coderd after Postgres**. DERP was not moving traffic (0 packets / 0 bytes) — the cost is **connection occupancy**, not relay throughput.

We did **not** take a Go pprof, so we cannot name a mutex or function. We *can* rule out “the box ran out of CPU/RAM” and “Postgres is slow.”

| Finding | Number |
| --- | --- |
| Last healthy idle plateau | **~200** connected workspaces |
| First failed plateau | **400** (`GET /api/v2/workspaces` p95 **480 ms** vs 300 ms) |
| Exact cliff | **Untested** (between 200 and 400) |
| Bigger control-plane CPU/RAM | **Would not have changed the 400 result** |
| More provisionerd (we used 5) | **Would not have changed the 400 result** |

600 and 800 were **not started** (ramp stops on first degraded hold).

