### What Prometheus showed (cause vs not-cause)

Scrapes of `coder-prom:2112` at the start and end of each idle hold (`prom-{N}-hold-*.prom`). Gauges below are the **end-of-hold** snapshot.

**The load that grew with N** (one of each per idle workspace, all terminated on this coderd — in-cluster ClusterIP, not public Tailscale DERP):

| Prometheus signal | At N=200 | At N=400 | Read as |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| `coderd_agents_up` | 200 | 400 | Agents still healthy; connectivity did not fail |
| `coderd_api_concurrent_websockets{path="/api/v2/workspaceagents/me/rpc"}` | 200 | 400 | Persistent agent control-plane RPC |
| `coderd_api_concurrent_requests{path="/derp"}` | 200 | 400 | Persistent HTTP to embedded DERP |
| `coder_derp_server_connections` / `clients_local` | 201 | 401 | Same DERP sockets counted on the server |
| `go_goroutines` | ~4.2k | ~8.2k | ~20 goroutines per agent |

**What Prometheus showed was *not* the problem:**

| Prometheus signal | At N=400 | Read as |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `coderd_api_request_latencies_seconds` for `GET /api/v2/templates` | p95 still **24 ms** | Other REST is fine |
| same histogram for `GET /healthz` | p95 **1 ms** | Process still serving |
| `coderd_provisionerd_jobs_current` | **0** | Not stuck in Terraform |
| `coderd_db_query_latencies_seconds` `GetAuthorizedWorkspaces` | mean **~11 ms** (almost all &lt; 25 ms) | Postgres is not the 480 ms |
| `coder_derp_server_bytes_*` / `packets_*` | **0** | DERP is idle; no relay throughput |
| `coder_derp_server_average_queue_duration_ms` | **0** | DERP queue not backed up |
| `process_cpu_seconds_total` over the 400 hold | ~0.18 cores | Not CPU-bound |
| `go_memstats_sys_bytes` | ~573 MiB | Not memory-bound |

`coderd_api_concurrent_requests` in the old summary scripts is a **sum across paths**. The 400 concurrent HTTP requests are **GET `/derp`**, not 400 overlapping workspace-list calls. The canary only listed workspaces about once every 2 seconds.

So the cause we can stand on: **one coderd process holding ~400 agent RPC websockets + ~400 idle DERP connections, then executing `GET /api/v2/workspaces` which joins DB rows with that in-memory agent state.** List p95 was flat 50→100 (49 ms), ~2× at 200 (97 ms), ~5× from 200 at 400 (480 ms). We did not profile the handler itself.

## Control-plane CPU and memory (not the limiter)

Live cgroup (still at 400 agents, after the degraded hold):

| Pod | CPU throttle | Memory peak | Limit |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| coderd | **7.4 s** throttled over the whole run (~1.4% of CFS periods) | **1.64 GiB** | 16 GiB |
| Postgres | **2.0 s** throttled | **~190 MiB** | 8 GiB |

coderd `process_cpu_seconds_total` **during the 600 s idle holds** (user+system):

| N | CPU-seconds in hold | Equivalent cores |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 50 | 15.6 | ~0.03 |
| 100 | 23.9 | ~0.04 |
| 200 | 44.8 | ~0.08 |
| 400 | 107.4 | ~0.18 |

Go `sys` bytes at 400 ≈ **573 MiB**. coderd RSS ≈ **640 MiB**. Control node allocatable ~7.9 CPU / 30 GiB; requests were ~54% CPU / 40% memory.

**Postgres is not the 480 ms.** `GetAuthorizedWorkspaces` (the list query) averaged **~11 ms** (2094 samples; almost all &lt; 25 ms). Extra latency is in coderd after the query (assemble 400 workspace+agent records while 400 DERP + 400 RPC connections are live).

## Provisionerd (not the limiter)

- 5 built-in daemons; **0 jobs** on every idle hold.
- Successful template STARTs: **375**, **all &lt; 10 s**, mean **~5.1 s** (`coderd_provisionerd_workspace_build_timings_seconds`).
- Fill concurrency was 5, matching daemon count, so we did not build a large provisioner queue.
- Extra in-process daemons would only help **burst-create** start latency. They share the coderd process on OSS; they would not unload idle agent connections.

## What this means for the product

1. **~400 ms (p95 ~480 ms) on workspace list is too much for this SLO.** We required p95 ≤ **300 ms**. At 400 idle agents the median was already 300 ms, so the common case is at the limit, not just the tail. Treat that plateau as over capacity for a control-plane / middle-service poll path, even if a single UI click would still “work.”
2. **Plan ~200 concurrent connected workspaces per OSS `coderd` (v2.34.5)** if that 300 ms p95 list SLO (or any handler that walks all live agents) still applies.
3. Agent jobs should be **ephemeral**: stop/delete when the run finishes so live agent count ≈ jobs in flight, not lifetime creates.
4. The **middle service should own job lists**. Do not use `GET /api/v2/workspaces` as a hot dashboard path. Talk to Coder by workspace id/name.
5. **Do not scale this with a larger `m7i` or more provisionerd.** Next capacity lever if you need &gt;200 in flight: **more `coderd` replicas** sharing Postgres (spread DERP + agent RPC), which this lab did **not** measure.
6. Kubernetes packing was fine: 400 workspace pods stayed Running; the miss is coderd, not the node groups.

## What we did not measure

- N = 250, 300, 350 (cliff is only bounded)
- `coderd` `replicaCount` &gt; 1
- Premium external provisioners
- Nested Podman / FUSE / workspace PVCs
- Active work inside the sandbox (Claude SDK, etc.) — only idle `coder_agent`
- HA / disk-backed Postgres
- API paths other than the canary trio (focus = workspace list)
- Go pprof / mutex profile of the workspace-list handler (Prometheus shows *where* time is not, not the exact function)

